{"step_index":8769,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-24T22:02:16Z","thinking":"The `NO_MATCH` status arises because of a commercial prefix mismatch test. The code checks if the presence of \"نيو\" or \"new\" (case-insensitive) differs between the request and candidate. Since one contains \"نيو\" (or its English equivalent) and the other does not, this triggers the mismatch condition, resulting in a negative match. This suggests a focus on specific commercial prefixes to distinguish between products.\n\n\nThe core issue is whether \"نيو\" functions as a distinct commercial prefix or is part of a product's base name. The current logic uses substring matching on \"نيو,\" creating a mismatch when \"نيوروفيت\" (which contains \"نيو\" in some spellings) is compared to \"نيروفيت\" (lacking \"و\"). This behavior is problematic since \"نيوروفيت\" and \"نيروفيت\" are, in this context, the same product.\n\n\nThe logic incorrectly treats \"نيو\" as a standalone prefix in all cases. The goal of `is_neo` was to identify the commercial prefix \"نيو\", distinct from the root product name. A substring check is insufficient to determine whether \"نيو\" is part of a product name. Examining the `has_co` regex, it appears to correctly identify \"co\" and \"plus\" as standalone terms. This contrasting behavior needs further investigation.\n\n\nThe substring check for \"نيو\" fails to distinguish between a prefix and part of a root word, leading to incorrect mismatches. Using regex to check for \"نيو\" as a standalone word (e.g., `re.search(r'(?:^|[^\\w])(نيو|new)(?:[^\\w]|$)', t)`) appears to be a more robust approach. The context of when this \"neo\" rule was added is necessary for debugging, specifically if this was done post-Gemini. This fix could possibly introduce new errors with terms such as `نيوكربون` because `كربون` is still a root term and should match in a real-world scenario.\n\n\nThe code's \"neo\" check, based on substring inclusion, is overly aggressive and leads to incorrect mismatches between closely related product names, as with \"نيوروفيت\" and \"نيروفيت\". This substring test causes mismatches even when Gemini considers the candidates a match, potentially discarding valid results due to a poorly defined prefix check. The inconsistency between this check and other regex-based prefix checks (e.g., \"co\") warrants further scrutiny. The \"neo\" rule needs revision to correctly identify commercial prefixes without unintentionally rejecting minor variations in product names.\n\n\nThe code's \"neo\" check, which uses substring matching, is overly strict, resulting in false negatives for products like \"نيوروفيت\" vs. \"نيروفيت.\" This rigid rule is affecting a range of products, including Neuroton and Neurobion, if the catalog uses a different spelling of \"نيو.\" Persian/Arabic OCR variations using `ی` (U+06CC) instead of `ي` (U+064A) are also incorrectly triggering mismatches, as demonstrated by the \"نيوكربون\" example. Therefore the root cause is due to incorrect handling of similar prefix names.\n\n\nBased on user instructions and observed behavior, several post-Gemini checks need review. Strength differences remain a definitive `NO_MATCH`.  Combination suffixes (like \"co,\" \"plus\") should remain. Dosage form differences now warrant a `REVIEW` status, not an outright rejection, for pharmacist decision-making.  The problematic `is_neo` check, causing false negatives, will be commented out, as Gemini already handles drug name variations correctly.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"\"D:\\\\AI_Engineer\\\\Pharmacy-agy\\\\src\\\\fast_match\\\\coordinator.py\"","EndLine":"445","StartLine":"418","toolAction":"\"Viewing file\"","toolSummary":"\"View neo check in coordinator.py\""}}]}
